# 0009. Codex proposes docs as file text from a read-only session

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-25

For docs, Codex returns the new text of each file in its reply instead of writing files.
The launcher from [0008](0008-codex-calls-go-through-one-launcher.adr) checks the paths and
builds the diff itself. After this, every Codex call this integration makes is read-only. Approved by the owner on 2026-11-25.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: [0006](0006-codex-read-only-second-agent.adr) gives Codex `workspace-write`
  in its own worktree for docs, and Claude rejects the diff if it touches anything but docs.
  Inside that worktree Codex can still write and run anything before the check. This is the
  last writable path in the integration.
- **Decision**: `codex.py docs --repo DIR`. It makes the same read-only call as `ask` (same
  fixed flags) and wraps the prompt in a fixed instruction: the final message is one JSON
  object, `{"files": [{"path": "<repo-relative>", "text": "<complete new content>"}]}`,
  nothing else, also enforced with `codex exec --output-schema`. Codex never writes Git patch
  metadata. The launcher builds the unified diff
  from the current file (or empty, for a new one) and Codex's text, with `difflib`.
- **Path rules**, checked by the launcher against the repo resolved from `--repo` once, at
  the start:
  - allowed (an allowlist, case-insensitive): `.md` files that are a `README.md` anywhere,
    sit at the repo root, or are under `docs/`;
  - denied, compared after case-folding (this Mac's checkout is case-insensitive):
    anything under `docs/adr/`; agent instruction files (`SKILL.md`, `CLAUDE.md`,
    `CLAUDE.local.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `AGENTS.override.md`, `GEMINI.md`), which steer agents
    and so count as code in this repo; anything under a `skills/`, `commands/` or `agents/`
    directory (plugin prompts are `.md` files); anything under a directory starting with `.`
    (`.github/`, `.claude-plugin/`, …); paths with control characters. An allowlist, not
    just a denylist, because the commit security review showed that a list of names always
    misses a kind of prompt file;
  - an existing file must be tracked under exactly that spelling (`git ls-files` returns the
    same name), so `readme.md` can't stand in for `README.md` on a case-insensitive disk;
  - each path must be relative, with no `..`, no empty or `.` parts, no backslash or NUL;
    listed once; and, after `realpath`, still inside the repo;
  - no part of the path may be a symlink. An existing target must be a regular file that is
    not executable and is tracked by git (`git --literal-pathspecs ls-files -z`);
  - allowed operations: change an existing file, or create a new `.md` file (new files get
    mode 644 when applied). Never delete, rename, or change a mode;
  - the JSON must parse, `files` must be non-empty, and `text` must be valid UTF-8 and at
    most 200 KB per file.
- **Output**: pass → the launcher writes a manifest to a private temp file (`mkstemp`,
  0600): the repo root and, per file, its path, the SHA-256 of its current content (none for
  a new file) and the new text. It prints the repo, the manifest path, the manifest's
  SHA-256, the files, and a unified diff of each one. The diff is for reading only. Fail →
  each reason on its own line, exit 2, nothing written anywhere. Codex unavailable → the skip
  line from 0008.
- **Claude's part**: show me the full diff (the 0006 rule to show Codex's output in full,
  redacting secrets, applies to the diff too). Then
  `python3 -I codex.py docs-apply --repo DIR --sha256 <hex> <manifest>`. It checks the
  manifest's SHA-256, that its repo is this repo, every path rule against the current tree,
  and that each file's content still matches the recorded SHA-256 (or is still absent).
  Only when everything passes does it write each file's exact text, then it deletes the
  manifest. Any failure → exit 2, nothing written. Then Claude commits and opens the PR
  (`github-flow`, `commit-style`). Codex never commits or pushes (unchanged).
- **Why a manifest, not `git apply`** (changed during implementation, after Codex's review):
  a patch handed to `git apply` can carry operations the path check never saw (deletes,
  other targets, names with control characters), and it applies cleanly even when the file
  changed outside its hunks. Writing the checked text directly, guarded by the recorded
  preimage hash, applies exactly what was shown and nothing else.
- **Self-test**: a clean edit and a new `.md` file both pass. Each of these fails with the
  right reason: `.py`, `docs/x.sh`, `DOCS/ADR/0001.adr`, `docs/adr/README.md`, `SKILL.md`
  anywhere, `CLAUDE.md`, `CLAUDE.local.md`, `AGENTS.override.md`, `GEMINI.md`, `sub/x.md`,
  `plugins/p/commands/x.md`, a `README.md` under `skills/`, `.github/x.md`, control
  characters in a path, a lone surrogate in the text, `../x.md`, an absolute path, a path through a
  symlinked dir, a symlink target, an executable target, an untracked existing file, a
  duplicate path, an empty list, invalid JSON, prose around the JSON. `docs-apply` refuses a
  changed manifest (SHA mismatch), another repo, and a file changed after the check, with
  no partial writes. CRLF line endings, U+2028 in text, a `README.md` in a subfolder, and a
  non-ASCII tracked name (`café.md`) all work. All run in a
  throwaway git repo.
- **Files**: `docs` and `docs-apply` in `codex.py` with their tests; the `codex` skill's
  `## Docs` rewritten; workflow minor bump; CHANGELOG. This replaces only 0006's
  "Write docs" bullet (writable worktree plus diff check); the rest of 0006 stands.
- **Consequences**: no writable Codex session anywhere in this integration. Docs become
  "file text → launcher checks the paths and builds the diff → I see it → Claude applies".
  Cost: Codex can't run a docs build or link checker while writing (it never had to), and
  agent instruction files and plugin prompts are out of Codex's reach, and so are
  `.md` files outside `README.md`, the repo root and `docs/`. Claude writes those. Large jobs go one file per call to stay within output limits.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: launcher from 0008, Python 3 stdlib (`json`, `difflib`, `hashlib`, `tempfile`),
  `git ls-files`.
- Alternatives:
  - Keep the writable worktree plus diff check (status quo): it only checks after the fact.
  - The launcher builds a patch and runs `git apply` (the first implementation): see "Why a
    manifest" above.
  - Codex returns a unified diff, and the launcher validates it before `git apply`: the
    validator would have to understand every kind of Git patch metadata (renames, copies,
    modes, binary), a much larger attack surface than path plus text.
  - Allow every file under `docs/`: it can hold scripts and build config, and that breaks
    "Codex never edits code".
  - Any `.md` file minus a denylist of names (the first draft): misses plugin prompts in
    `commands/` and `agents/`, and whatever the next tool names its instruction file.
  - Codex writes nothing, and Claude writes docs from Codex's notes: this drops the docs role
    0006 gave Codex.
